Task 1: Preparing the System for IdM Installation
Subtask 1.1: System Updates and Package Installation
First, ensure your system is up-to-date and install the necessary packages for IdM server deployment.

Update the system packages:
sudo dnf update -y
Install the IdM server packages:
sudo dnf install -y ipa-server ipa-server-dns
Verify package installation:
rpm -qa | grep ipa-server
Expected Output: You should see packages like ipa-server and ipa-server-dns listed.

Subtask 1.2: Hostname and Network Configuration
Proper hostname and network configuration is critical for IdM server functionality.

Set a fully qualified domain name (FQDN):
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm.example.com
Verify hostname configuration:
hostnamectl status
hostname -f
Update the hosts file for proper name resolution:
sudo vi /etc/hosts
Add the following line (replace with your actual IP address):

192.168.1.100    idm.example.com    idm
Test hostname resolution:
ping -c 3 idm.example.com
nslookup idm.example.com
Subtask 1.3: Firewall Configuration
Configure the firewall to allow IdM services to communicate properly.

Check current firewall status:
sudo firewall-cmd --state
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
Add IdM services to firewall:
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=freeipa-ldap --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=freeipa-ldaps --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=dns --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=ntp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=https --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=kerberos --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=kpasswd --permanent
Reload firewall configuration:
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Verify firewall rules:
sudo firewall-cmd --list-services
Task 2: Installing and Configuring the IdM Server
Subtask 2.1: Running the IdM Server Installation
Now we'll install and configure the IdM server with integrated DNS and Kerberos services.

Start the IdM server installation with DNS integration:
sudo ipa-server-install --setup-dns --auto-forwarders
Important: This command will prompt you for several configuration options. Here's what to expect:

Configuration Prompts and Responses:
When prompted, provide the following information:

Server host name: Accept the default (idm.example.com) or enter your FQDN
Domain name: Enter example.com
Realm name: Accept the default (EXAMPLE.COM) - this will be uppercase
Directory Manager password: Create a strong password (minimum 8 characters)
IPA admin password: Create a strong password (minimum 8 characters)
DNS forwarders: Accept auto-detected forwarders or specify custom ones
Reverse DNS zone: Accept the suggested reverse zone
Example Installation Command with Parameters:

sudo ipa-server-install \
  --setup-dns \
  --domain=example.com \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --ds-password=SecurePassword123 \
  --admin-password=AdminPassword123 \
  --auto-forwarders \
  --auto-reverse \
  --unattended
Subtask 2.2: Monitoring Installation Progress
The installation process typically takes 10-15 minutes. Monitor the progress and watch for any error messages.

Installation Progress Indicators:

Package installation and configuration
Certificate authority setup
Directory server configuration
Kerberos KDC setup
DNS server configuration
Web server setup
Successful Installation Indicators:

Look for messages similar to:

The ipa-server-install command was successful
Setup complete

Next steps:
1. You must make sure these network ports are open:
   TCP Ports:
     * 80, 443: HTTP/HTTPS
     * 389, 636: LDAP/LDAPS
     * 88, 464: kerberos
   UDP Ports:
     * 88, 464: kerberos
     * 53: bind
2. You can now obtain a kerberos ticket using the command: 'kinit admin'
   This ticket will allow you to use the IPA tools (e.g., ipa user-add)
   and the web user interface.
Subtask 2.3: Post-Installation System Configuration
After successful installation, configure the system for optimal IdM operation.

Enable and start IdM services:
sudo systemctl enable ipa
sudo systemctl status ipa
Configure automatic startup:
sudo systemctl list-unit-files | grep ipa
Set up Kerberos authentication:
kinit admin
Enter the admin password you created during installation.

Verify Kerberos ticket:
klist
Expected Output: You should see a valid Kerberos ticket for admin@EXAMPLE.COM.

Task 3: Verifying Installation and Server Status
Subtask 3.1: Service Status Verification
Verify that all IdM components are running correctly.

Check IdM service status:
sudo ipactl status
Expected Output:

Directory Service: RUNNING
krb5kdc Service: RUNNING
kadmin Service: RUNNING
named Service: RUNNING
httpd Service: RUNNING
ipa-custodia Service: RUNNING
pki-tomcatd Service: RUNNING
ipa-otpd Service: RUNNING
ipa-dnskeysyncd Service: RUNNING
ipa: INFO: The ipactl command was successful
Check individual service status:
sudo systemctl status dirsrv@EXAMPLE-COM.service
sudo systemctl status krb5kdc.service
sudo systemctl status kadmin.service
sudo systemctl status named.service
sudo systemctl status httpd.service
Subtask 3.2: DNS Configuration Verification
Verify that DNS services are properly configured and responding.

Test DNS resolution:
nslookup idm.example.com
dig idm.example.com
Test reverse DNS lookup:
nslookup 192.168.1.100
dig -x 192.168.1.100
Verify DNS server is listening:
sudo netstat -tulpn | grep :53
sudo ss -tulpn | grep :53
Subtask 3.3: Web Interface Access Verification
Test access to the IdM web management interface.

Check web server status:
sudo systemctl status httpd
curl -k https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/
Access the web interface:
Open a web browser and navigate to:

https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/
Login credentials:
Username: admin
Password: The admin password you set during installation
Subtask 3.4: LDAP Directory Verification
Verify that the LDAP directory is properly configured and accessible.

Test LDAP connectivity:
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" -s base
Query user information:
ipa user-show admin
List available IPA commands:
ipa help
ipa help user
Subtask 3.5: Certificate Authority Verification
Verify that the Certificate Authority is properly configured.

Check CA status:
sudo systemctl status pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat.service
Verify certificate installation:
ipa-cacert-manage -p /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ -t CT,C,C install /etc/ipa/ca.crt
Test certificate validation:
openssl x509 -in /etc/ipa/ca.crt -text -noout
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Installation Fails Due to Hostname Resolution
Problem: Installation fails with DNS resolution errors.

Solution:

# Verify hostname configuration
hostname -f
# Update /etc/hosts file
sudo vi /etc/hosts
# Add proper FQDN entry
Issue 2: Firewall Blocking Services
Problem: Services cannot communicate due to firewall restrictions.

Solution:

# Check firewall status
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
# Add missing services
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=freeipa-ldap --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Issue 3: Kerberos Authentication Failures
Problem: Cannot obtain Kerberos tickets.

Solution:

# Check time synchronization
sudo chrony sources
# Verify realm configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf
# Restart Kerberos services
sudo systemctl restart krb5kdc kadmin
Issue 4: Web Interface Not Accessible
Problem: Cannot access the IdM web interface.

Solution:

# Check Apache status
sudo systemctl status httpd
# Verify SSL certificates
sudo openssl x509 -in /etc/httpd/alias/server.crt -text -noout
# Restart web services
sudo systemctl restart httpd
